rsx draw engine + metal: a Z16 zeta read as DEPTH16 (a shadow map) is converted on the GPU, not stored through guest memory.

Resistance 3 renders its shadow maps into Z16 zetas (416 to 992 square, pitch 2 * width, each at its own address
from 0x01DA8800 on) and samples one or two of them a frame as a linear unnormalized DEPTH16 texture of the zeta's own
pitch, with the shadow compare. The engine served each read through eng_zdepth_to_guest: submit_and_wait (the FIFO
thread waits for the GPU), a readback, a CPU store, then a hash, a decode and an upload of up to 2 MB, about 140 MB
per 120 frames.

gcm-rsx/0012's backend hook becomes depth_as_color(depth, w, h, rsx_fmt): 0x85 keeps the A8R8G8B8 bytes of a Z24S8
zeta, 0x92 makes an R16 unorm texture of round(depth * 0xFFFF) for a Z16 zeta, any other format 0. The Metal backend
records one pass for either (ENG_REC_DEPTH_COLOR; the destination's pixel format picks the pipeline), both
fragment functions in the one 64-bit-integer library, rounding exactly. The unit samples a surface_view of it with
its crossbar, which swizzles as texture_create does, so the shader's shadow compare reads the values the decode gave.
eng_zdepth_as_color takes DEPTH16 only for a Z16 zeta and A8R8G8B8 only for a Z24S8 one; one conversion is kept per
zeta, keyed on the depth write, the size and the format. Anything else, a backend without the hook, or
PS3RECOMP_ENG_DEPTH_VIA_GUEST=1 goes through guest memory as before.
The CPU store of a Z16 zeta now rounds d * 65535 in double, as the Z24S8 one does: in float the product rounds first
and a depth within 0.002 of a half could land on the other value (0.501953125 stored 0x8080, not 0x807F).

test_rsx_draw_engine, new test_z16_read_as_depth16_on_gpu: the read goes to the hook with format 0x92, with no
readback, store or upload; the same texture until the next depth write; Z16 as A8R8G8B8 and Z24S8 as DEPTH16 take
the store; a refusing backend stores round(d * 0xFFFF) (fails with the float rounding). With
PS3RECOMP_ENG_DEPTH_VIA_GUEST=1 13 checks fail.
test_metal_depth_as_argb.m is now test_metal_depth_as_color.m: the DEPTH16 conversion of the same depths, plus
values either side of 16-bit rounding ties, matches readback + Z16 store + rsx_texture_decode byte for byte, and its
view samples as the uploaded R16 texture (both fail with the float formula in the shader); 21 checks.

Upstream: not proposed yet (extends PR #499: the hook it adds becomes depth_as_color; to follow it).

